.TH GIT-EZ 1 "November 21, 2026" "git-ez" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-ez \- local rebase and merge with AI-assisted conflict resolution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git ez rebase
.I target
.RI [ --onto " newbase"]
.RI [ --repo " directory"]
.RI [ --check " executable arguments..."]
.br
.B git ez merge
.I target
.RI [ --ff " | " --no-ff " | " --ff-only ]
.RI [ --repo " directory"]
.RI [ --check " executable arguments..."]
.br
.B git ez probe --output
.I report.json
.br
.B git ez show
.I run-directory
.br
.B git ez --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Integrate a revision into the current branch using Git, calling a configured
LLM
when conflicts require resolution. Preserve an audit of changes, decisions,
and reasons. No branches or worktrees are created; no fetch or push is
performed.
.PP
A checked-out branch, clean checkout, and no active Git operation are
required.
The target is required and pinned at startup. Only simple rebase and merge are
supported; arbitrary Git options are not forwarded. Interactive rebase,
rebasing merge commits, merge --squash, --no-commit, octopus
merge,
and unrelated histories are unsupported. The tool cannot attach to an
operation
already started with Git.
.PP
Only one git-ez integration can run per checkout. A concurrent invocation fails
immediately, before repository checks, audit creation, or model calls. The lock
covers review, validation, and final audit capture. Linked worktrees have
independent locks. Native Git and other tools do not honor this advisory lock.
.PP
The lock is released when the process exits, even after abrupt termination.
The empty ez-execution.lock file remains in the checkout's Git directory;
its presence does not indicate an active run. Do not delete it during a run.
Git operations and local changes are preserved for inspection and native recovery.
.PP
Rebase uses the merge backend with autostash, update-refs, and rebase-merges
disabled. Merge uses --no-edit and respects Git's fast-forward policy. Git
runs
noninteractively with diff3 conflict markers. Hooks and signing are respected.
Caller configuration is otherwise retained, except repository-redirection
environment variables.
.PP
Valid model configuration is required even when no conflicts occur; no model
call is made when Git resolves the operation without assistance.
.SH INSTALLATION ON FREEBSD
FreeBSD support is experimental. CI targets FreeBSD 15.1 amd64/Python 3.12 only.
ARM64 is outside the CI scope. This in-memory checkpoint experiment requires
its own FreeBSD 15.1 amd64 CI validation.
Install git, uv, python312, py312-sqlite3, rust, and pkgconf from FreeBSD packages.
Use uv tool install with --python /usr/local/bin/python3.12 and the repository
URL or local checkout. The environment remains isolated from system Python
packages. Dependencies without FreeBSD wheels may compile from source.
Graph checkpoints stay in memory; no SQLite checkpoint adapter or sqlite-vec
is required. Events and offloaded agent files are retained in the audit directory.
Internal graph states and pending tasks are not persisted for inspection or restart.
Abrupt termination may leave incomplete audit files and skips final capture.
See the README for commands and current validation limits.
Development requirements and FreeBSD-specific tool warnings are documented in
CONTRIBUTING.md. Ruff is not required at runtime.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --keep-base, --no-keep-base
Rebase only. Keep the unique common ancestor with the upstream as the result's
base, or reset this policy. Implies --reapply-cherry-picks unless explicitly
disabled. Incompatible with --onto. Missing or ambiguous merge bases are rejected.
The upstream tip need not be an ancestor of a successful result in this mode.
.TP
.B --fork-point, --no-fork-point
Rebase only. Use the named upstream reference's local reflog to refine replay
selection, or disable this policy. The last explicit CLI value wins. With the
required explicit upstream, fork-point defaults to disabled, including with
--keep-base or rebase.forkPoint=true. Like native Git, that configuration does
not enable fork-point for an explicit upstream. Use --fork-point to opt in.
Requires a named branch, remote-tracking branch, tag or HEAD. Use --no-fork-point
for a literal SHA or revision expression. An unavailable reflog point falls back
to the otherwise chosen replay boundary, with an explicit audit reason.
.PP
The engine pins the base and fork point before integration and passes explicit
--onto and --no-fork-point arguments. Divergent exclusion histories may require
an internal merge commit object to preserve native cherry-pick equivalence.
No branch or reference is created for this object and it is excluded from the
result. It is unsigned metadata; signing policy still applies to result commits.
The audit records its parents, tree, command and raw capture, with any
truncation explicit. Native pre-rebase hooks receive this effective pinned replay
boundary rather than the original upstream name. Hook failures are not bypassed.
Hooks requiring the original upstream argument should use native Git directly.
.PP
When --onto equals the fork point and combining exclusions changes the replay
boundary, the native interactive sequencer avoids an incorrect up-to-date shortcut.
A capture-only editor records its instructions without changing them. Autosquash
remains disabled unless explicitly requested; the default empty policy remains
drop and per-commit reuse is preserved. The instruction-format override and
bounded sequence capture described below also apply to this case.
.PP
Original input references remain monitored. Final ancestry and range-diff use the
computed result base and replay selection. These options combine with autosquash
and signing; --root and rebasing merge commits remain unsupported.
.TP
.B --autosquash, --no-autosquash
Rebase only. Opt in to native grouping of fixup!, squash!, and amend! commits,
or disable it. The last explicit value wins. rebase.autoSquash alone does not
activate this mode. Git chooses the order and combined messages, with no editor
or model intervention except for resolving conflicts.
.PP
git-ez uses interactive machinery internally with a capture-only sequence editor,
forces rebase.instructionFormat=%s for this invocation, and preserves the default
--empty=drop. Explicit empty policies apply to replayed commits; Git handles empty
fixup/squash resolutions within their existing groups. Interactive editing remains
unsupported. Hooks and signing are retained.
.PP
The audit captures rebase-todo.txt before replay. A failed or over-limit capture
stops Git before replay; missing evidence prevents overall success after completion.
The capture editor records rebase-todo.txt.sha256 before replay. Changes to the
sequence or fingerprint, including from Git hooks, make the run fail.
rebase-commits.log records final commits and messages with explicit truncation at
ez.maxFileBytes. Local state and available evidence are preserved on failure.
.TP
.B --rerere-autoupdate, --no-rerere-autoupdate
Override rerere.autoupdate for the whole run, including continuation, without
changing stored configuration. Does not enable rerere.enabled. Resolutions reused
and staged by native rerere are audited and continued without a model call for
that conflict. With --no-rerere-autoupdate, reused file contents still pass through
assisted resolution and validation while the index remains unmerged. The audit
records requested and effective autoupdate.
.TP
.B -m message, --message message
Merge only. Supply the merge commit message. Repeat for additional paragraphs.
Mutually exclusive with --file. Does not force a commit on fast-forward.
.TP
.B -F file, --file file
Merge only. Read a UTF-8 message file before integration. Paths are relative to
the invocation directory, including with --repo. Standard input (-F -) is unsupported.
The message is recorded in the audit and retained after conflicts.
.TP
.B --log[=n], --no-log
Merge only. Include up to n incoming commit subjects in the message. Bare --log
uses 20; attach another nonnegative limit with --log=5. --no-log means --log=0.
The last explicit value wins. If omitted, merge.log applies. Git generates the
summary, including with -m or -F; it survives conflict continuation.
.TP
.B --cleanup=strip|whitespace|verbatim|scissors|default
Merge only. Apply Git's native cleanup mode to the message, also after conflicts.
The prepared MERGE_MSG is preserved during continuation. No editor is opened;
scissors retains native noninteractive behavior. Omission uses native defaults
and configuration. Neither cleanup nor log forces a commit on fast-forward.
Requested policies are recorded in the audit.
.TP
.B -S[key], --gpg-sign[=key], --no-gpg-sign
Sign newly created commits or explicitly disable signing. Attach an optional key
to the option; without one, Git's configured key is used. The policy remains
effective after conflicts and when retaining empty resolutions. Signing failures
are not bypassed. Existing commits reused by Git are not signed again.
.TP
.B --signoff, --no-signoff
Add or omit the committer's Signed-off-by trailer on newly created commits.
This is distinct from a cryptographic signature. Hooks remain active.
.TP
.B --verify-signatures, --no-verify-signatures
Merge only. Verify the incoming tip commit with native Git trust settings, or
explicitly disable this verification. This does not verify every incoming commit
or an annotated tag's signature. Explicit options override Git configuration.
.TP
.B --empty=drop|keep|stop
Rebase only. Control commits that become empty. Explicit policies also apply
after assisted conflict resolution: keep creates an empty commit with the original
author and message, drop lets Git discard it, and stop leaves the active rebase
for manual handling with a nonzero exit code. This extends native continuation,
which can drop an empty resolution even with --empty=keep. Hooks and signing
remain active. Policies and empty resolutions are recorded in the audit.
.TP
.B --keep-empty, --no-keep-empty
Rebase only. Keep or discard commits that were already empty before replay.
.TP
.B --reapply-cherry-picks, --no-reapply-cherry-picks
Rebase only. Control whether Git replays changes equivalent to upstream commits.
Reapplied commits that become empty are handled by --empty.
.TP
.B --force-rebase, -f, --no-ff, --ff
For rebase, force replay of unchanged commits; --ff resets this request. The last
request wins. For merge, --ff and --no-ff instead select the policy described below.
.TP
.B --onto newbase
Rebase only. Replay the commits selected after target onto newbase. Both revisions
are pinned and their named references are checked throughout the run. The audit
and range-diff record the upstream boundary and new base separately. Only the
current branch is rebased. A second positional branch argument and the A...B
new-base shorthand are unsupported.
.TP
.B --ff, --no-ff, --ff-only
Merge only. Allow fast-forward with a merge commit otherwise (--ff), create a
merge commit when integration is needed (--no-ff), or refuse divergent histories
(--ff-only). An already up-to-date branch stays unchanged. An explicit policy
overrides Git configuration; the last policy specified wins. Without one, Git
configuration and defaults apply. A refused --ff-only merge does not call the
model or change the checkout. The requested policy is recorded in the audit and
report. Specify these options before --check.
.TP
.B --repo directory
Use this repository instead of the current directory. Subdirectories are
accepted.
.TP
.B --check executable arguments...
Run final validation without a shell at the repository root. Must be the last
option. The agent may also run this command during resolution.
.TP
.B -h
Display command-line usage. Each subcommand also accepts this option.
.TP
.B --help
Display this manual through Git. Requires the manual directory on MANPATH.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Use native Git configuration keys under ez.*, with normal Git scopes and
includes.
Set ez.model to the exact model identifier. The default provider is openai,
using a Chat Completions-compatible endpoint; ollama is also supported.
.PP
Set ez.endpoint for a custom server, ez.apiKeyEnv for a token environment
variable,
and ez.caBundle for an internal PEM CA bundle. Prefer environment variables to
ez.apiKey, which Git stores in plaintext. HTTPS is required outside loopback
unless ez.allowInsecureHttp is explicitly enabled.
.PP
See README.md and examples/ez.gitconfig in the project repository for the full
configuration reference. Use probe to check native tool-call compatibility
without exposing a repository or shell tools.
.SH AUDIT AND FAILURE RECOVERY
Audits are stored under ez/runs/<id> in the repository's common Git directory.
Each run prints the report location. Use git ez show with that run directory.
.PP
On failure or interruption, local state is retained without automatic
rollback.
Inspect the report and git status; continue or abort an active operation using
native Git commands (git rebase --continue / --abort or git merge --continue /
--abort). If integration completed but final validation failed, there is no
active
operation to continue or abort. There is no agent session resume command.
.PP
The agent shell has host-process privileges and is not a system sandbox.
Content checks apply to conflicted files and supporting files changed by the agent.
Regular files integrated by Git without conflict are exempt when their contents and
permissions remain unchanged during resolution, including binary and oversized
resources. Audit file capture limits still apply.
.PP
Failure to write a required audit snapshot, including failure of Git commands
that capture diffs and enumerate files, fails the run. Partial evidence is retained
and the failure appears in each summary that can still be written. A completed
Git integration remains available locally. Secondary capture errors do not replace
the original failure reason. Final captures and summaries are attempted independently.
If summaries cannot be finalized, the CLI reports the outcome, audit errors, and
audit location; existing summary files can be stale or incomplete.
.PP
The manifest records the primary failure phase, type and message separately from
audit_errors. Evidence availability is available, partial, or unavailable for the
tracked final captures and summaries. Partial captures include truncation, missing
file contents, and artifacts that could not be finalized. This is not a complete
inventory of all audit files. Missing ordinary model/tool events stop the agent
as an audit failure; subsequent writes do not make that journal complete again.
Handled initialization failures mark the session failed before any model call;
remaining captures are attempted even if the folder or runtime metadata is missing.
The terminal session event includes the session metadata capture outcome.
Unexpected exceptions are re-raised unchanged after cleanup; the audit retains
their type and outcome without arbitrary exception messages.
.PP
Validation status distinguishes not_requested, not_run, running, passed, failed,
timed_out, and interrupted. A passing validation command does not imply overall
success if its output cannot be captured or a later invariant check fails.
.PP
Audit files may contain sensitive source code and tool output. Model
confidence
and passing tests do not prove semantic correctness.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful integration, displayed report, or compatible probe.
.TP
.B 1
Failed or interrupted run, or failed probe.
.TP
.B 2
Configuration, command-line, or precondition error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
git config --global ez.model exact-model-id
git config --global ez.endpoint https://ai-server.example/v1
git ez probe --output /tmp/ai-compatibility.json
git ez rebase main
git ez merge feature
git ez rebase main --check python -B -m pytest
.fi
